    <p class="lede first reveal">You didn't start a business to become its worst-paid employee. But that is what happens, quietly, somewhere around month three. The business works, a little, and the reward for it working is more of you. More posting, more sending, more replying, more checking whether the thing you posted actually went out.</p>
    <p class="reveal">Every task you do by hand is a task you have hired yourself to do forever.</p>
    <p class="reveal">Here is the sentence I want you to sit with: <strong>doing it by hand isn't discipline. It's a bottleneck.</strong> Discipline is building the thing once so it runs a thousand times. The women who get past the solo ceiling are not working more hours than you. They stopped selling their hours to their own business.</p>
    <p class="reveal">When this guide first went up, I told you that nothing in my business ran while I slept. That was true in July. It is not true now. Today a lesson publishes itself at eight every morning, every unanswered comment gets found three times a day, eighteen posts a day go out on a platform whose week was written in one sitting on Sunday, and every Friday the numbers from every channel land on one screen. So this is no longer a map drawn from the outside. It is the machine, opened up: what to automate first, what it looks like running, how to write the instruction so it survives your absence, and why paid traffic belongs in the same conversation.</p>

