    <div class="chapter reveal"><span class="bignum">01</span><div class="num">Chapter One</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="means">What automation actually means when you're a team of one</h2></div>
    <p class="answer reveal">Business automation, for a team of one, means a decision you make once that carries itself out every time without you.</p>
    <p class="reveal">Say the word and most women picture one of two things: enterprise software with a sales call and a four-figure invoice, or an influencer's forty-seven-app stack held together with tape and a virtual assistant in another time zone. Both pictures are wrong, and both are expensive. The first costs money. The second costs you, every week, in maintenance.</p>
    <p class="reveal">A welcome email that sends itself is automation. A post your platform's own scheduler publishes while you sleep is automation. A checkout that delivers your file at three in the morning to a customer you will never meet is automation. None of it needs code. Most of it doesn't even need a new subscription.</p>
    <p class="reveal">It needs you to notice a decision you keep re-making, and refuse to make it again.</p>
    <div class="box tip reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✦</span> The Edit</span><p>If you have done a task the same way five times, the sixth time is a hiring decision. You are either hiring a system, or you are re-hiring yourself.</p></div>
    <div class="box mistake reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✕</span> Common Mistake</span><p>Automating a business that doesn't work yet. Automation multiplies whatever is already true. If your offer doesn't convert, automation delivers that disappointment faster and to more people. Get one thing working by hand first. <em>Then</em> take your hands off it.</p></div>

    <div class="chapter left reveal"><span class="bignum">02</span><div class="num">Chapter Two</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="first">What should you automate first in your online business?</h2></div>
    <p class="answer reveal">Automate delivery first, the handoff from checkout to inbox, because a sale that doesn't deliver becomes a refund. Automate email second, a welcome sequence that greets every subscriber the moment she joins. Automate publishing third. Leave anything that speaks for you in public, replies, community, refunds, until last, and even then let the machine draft while you decide.</p>
    <p class="reveal">That is the order. Here is how to score your own list against it, because your business has tasks mine doesn't, and because most people automate whatever annoys them most. Annoyance is a terrible way to set priorities. The task that irritates you is rarely the task that is costing you.</p>
    <p class="reveal"><strong>Frequency.</strong> How often do you do it? Daily beats weekly beats monthly. A daily ten-minute task is sixty hours a year. A monthly annoyance is an afternoon.</p>
    <p class="reveal"><strong>Sameness.</strong> Do you do it the same way every time? If you could write the steps on an index card and hand them to a stranger, it is ready. If it needs you to read the room, it isn't ready to run alone, though it may be ready to be drafted.</p>
    <p class="reveal"><strong>Stakes.</strong> What happens if it runs wrong for a week before you notice? A post going out an hour late is a shrug. A checkout that stops delivering is a fire. A reply in your name that you would never have written is worse than both.</p>
    <p class="reveal">Score your tasks on those three and the winners almost always land in the order above: high frequency, perfectly repeatable, and failures you would hear about. For nearly every woman reading this, the top scorer is the welcome sequence.</p>
    <p class="deeper reveal"><span>Go deeper —</span> <a href="/learn/email-marketing-for-beginners">How to Write Your First 5 Emails →</a></p>

    <div class="chapter reveal"><span class="bignum">03</span><div class="num">Chapter Three</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="running">What actually runs inside this business now</h2></div>
    <p class="answer reveal">Five systems run here without me pressing a button: a morning publishing task, a reply sweep, a weekly batch of posts with a nightly loader, email sequences built inside a free plan's limits, and a weekly numbers read.</p>
    <p class="reveal">I would rather you trust this guide than be impressed by it, so here is each one with its rule, including the parts that are deliberately <em>not</em> automated.</p>
    <div class="framework reveal">
      <span class="tag"><span>✦</span> The Machine, In Order Of The Day</span>
      <div class="flow">
        <div class="node"><div class="h">8:00 AM</div><div class="t">The day's lesson publishes and posts itself.</div></div>
        <div class="arrow">→</div>
        <div class="node"><div class="h">3× a day</div><div class="t">The reply sweep finds every unanswered comment.</div></div>
        <div class="arrow">→</div>
        <div class="node"><div class="h">Every night</div><div class="t">Tomorrow's posts load into the scheduler.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="reveal"><strong>The morning task that publishes without me.</strong> At eight every morning a scheduled task publishes the day's lesson and posts it to the community. The distinction that matters is between a scheduled <em>post</em> and a scheduled <em>task</em>. A scheduled post is one finished thing waiting for a time. A scheduled task is an instruction that goes and does a job: finds today's material, puts it where it belongs, checks it landed. The second one is the one that gives you your mornings back.</p>
    <p class="reveal"><strong>The reply sweep.</strong> Three times a day, a task goes through every platform looking for anything addressed to me that nobody has answered, and drafts a reply into one dashboard. On the community platforms, nothing it writes goes out until I have read it. A separate task, running on its own clock, is the only thing allowed to press send. That split is the whole design. The drafter reads and writes. The sender sends. Neither does the other's job. I track response rate for the same reason I track revenue: a comment that sits unanswered for two days tells a new member exactly how much she matters here.</p>
    <p class="reveal"><strong>Eighteen posts a day, written on Sunday.</strong> On one platform I run a fixed grid of eighteen slots a day. Nobody writes eighteen posts a day. The week gets written in one sitting on Sunday, and a nightly loader tops up the platform's own scheduler with tomorrow's posts. The loader is kept deliberately stupid: it copies what is written into the slot it belongs in, and it does nothing clever. When a stupid loader fails, it fails loudly. When a clever one fails, you find out a week later.</p>
    <p class="reveal"><strong>Email inside real limits.</strong> My email platform's free plan allows three active automations. Not thirty. Three. That constraint turned out to be a gift, because every sequence has to earn its slot. A five-day challenge that walks a stranger through a real build earns one. A clever little follow-up that nobody would miss does not. Doorways beat garnishes. If your plan has a ceiling, the ceiling is your editor.</p>
    <p class="reveal"><strong>The weekly numbers.</strong> Once a week a task opens every dashboard the business has, reads the handful of numbers that matter, and writes them onto one screen with a chart that shows the trend. At least one of them is a leading number: participation, the one that moves before cancellations do. Here is the rule that makes it trustworthy. If the task can't read a number, it leaves a gap and says why. It never guesses, and it never carries last week's figure forward. A dashboard wired straight into every app is beautiful for a month and then quietly breaks, and you keep reading a number that stopped updating in week six. A gap you can see is more honest than a number you can't trust.</p>

    <div class="detail reveal">
      <div class="photo mask img" style="background-image:url(/images/library/detail-hands-typing-cinematic.jpg);background-size:cover;background-position:center"></div>
      <div class="say"><div class="k">What stayed human</div><p>Anything that speaks for me in a room of real people. The machine finds, drafts and reminds. I decide.</p></div>
    </div>

    <p class="reveal">Notice what is missing from that list: judgment. The machine does not decide which offer runs this week, what a member really meant, or whether a joke lands. It does the finding, the drafting, the loading and the checking. The moment a reader can tell she is talking to a robot, you have spent trust to save minutes, and that is the worst trade in this business.</p>

    <div class="chapter left reveal"><span class="bignum">04</span><div class="num">Chapter Four</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="instruction">How to write an instruction that survives your absence</h2></div>
    <p class="answer reveal">A good automation instruction names what to read first, states what finished looks like, checks its own result, and says what to do when something goes wrong.</p>
    <p class="reveal">Every scheduled task in this business is, underneath, a page of plain English. No code. The quality of the automation is the quality of that page. Here is what I have learned writing them, mostly by getting it wrong first.</p>
    <p class="reveal"><strong>Name the sources, in order.</strong> The task reads the price list before it writes a price, and the schedule before it books a slot. An instruction that says "use the right link" will eventually use a link it remembers. An instruction that says "read the price list, then use the link printed there" won't.</p>
    <p class="reveal"><strong>Say what done looks like.</strong> Not "post the week." Three posts a day, at these three times, in these categories, with no duplicates. If you can't describe done, the task can't reach it, and neither could you.</p>
    <p class="reveal"><strong>Make it check its own work.</strong> This is the rule that matters most, because the failure mode of automation is silence, not an error message. I have watched a platform report a successful save and store nothing. A task that says "done" without reading the result back is reporting what it meant to do. Every task here reloads, reads back and reports what it actually found.</p>
    <p class="reveal"><strong>Tell it what to do when it can't.</strong> The account isn't logged in, the file for today doesn't exist, the platform shows a warning. The right answer is almost always the same: stop, don't guess, and say exactly what you saw.</p>
    <div class="box mistake reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✕</span> The rule I learned the expensive way</span><p>One account, one automation at a time. Two tasks acting on the same account in the same window look, to the platform, exactly like the abuse pattern it exists to stop. Stagger them, give them their own hours, and never let a second one start while the first could still be running.</p></div>

    <div class="chapter reveal"><span class="bignum">05</span><div class="num">Chapter Five</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="stack">The stack: fewer tools than you think</h2></div>
    <p class="answer reveal">You probably don't need a connector app. Most of the automation a business of one needs already lives inside the tools it pays for.</p>
    <p class="reveal">For months, the automation lesson inside my own membership taught Make and Zapier. It was well written and thoroughly structured, and it taught a stack I don't use. So I deleted it, along with five other lessons built on tools this business has never run on, and rebuilt the module around the systems you just read about.</p>
    <p class="reveal">Here is what does the work instead, and none of it is exotic:</p>
    <div class="compare reveal">
      <div class="ttl">Who does what</div>
      <div class="cards">
        <div class="ccard"><div class="n">Built in</div><div class="row"><span class="k">The shop</span><span class="v">Delivers the file the moment someone pays</span></div><div class="row"><span class="k">Email platform</span><span class="v">Runs the welcome and challenge sequences</span></div><div class="row"><span class="k">Native schedulers</span><span class="v">Publish posts and pins on the platform itself</span></div></div>
        <div class="ccard"><div class="n">Added</div><div class="row"><span class="k">Scheduled AI tasks</span><span class="v">Anything that needs reading and writing: the sweep, the loader, the morning publish</span></div><div class="row"><span class="k">One dashboard</span><span class="v">Where drafts wait for approval and numbers live</span></div><div class="row"><span class="k">Rule</span><span class="v">Nothing outside the named stack</span></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="reveal">The rule on the bottom right is the one I would tattoo on a beginner. Every tool you add is a tool that can change its pricing, break a connection, or quietly stop firing. A small stack you understand beats a big one you are afraid to touch.</p>
    <p class="reveal">If two of your tools genuinely have to talk, and neither can do it alone, then add a connector. Not before. The women who lose this game spend three weeks comparing automation platforms, by hand, one browser tab at a time.</p>

    <div class="chapter left reveal"><span class="bignum">06</span><div class="num">Chapter Six</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="traffic">Why paid traffic lives in this pillar</h2></div>
    <p class="answer reveal">Paid traffic belongs beside automation because ads put arrivals on a schedule, and automation handles what happens after they arrive.</p>
    <p class="reveal">This surprises people. Why are ads in the automation guide? Because they are the same discipline. Organic reach is the manual labour of building an audience: unpredictable, dependent on an algorithm's mood, and priced in your hours. A tested ad is a tap with a dial. You are buying back the most expensive thing in your business, which was never money. It was waiting.</p>
    <div class="framework reveal">
      <span class="tag"><span>✦</span> The $10/Day Traffic Machine</span>
      <div class="flow">
        <div class="node"><div class="h">Ad</div><div class="t">One ad, cold traffic, fixed daily budget.</div></div>
        <div class="arrow">→</div>
        <div class="node"><div class="h">Landing Page</div><div class="t">One page, one free resource, one promise.</div></div>
        <div class="arrow">→</div>
        <div class="node"><div class="h">List</div><div class="t">The welcome sequence from Chapter Two.</div></div>
        <div class="arrow">→</div>
        <div class="node"><div class="h">Offer</div><div class="t">The thing you already sold by hand.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="box action reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">→</span> Free Resource</span><h3>The $10/Day Traffic Machine — Starter Map</h3><p>The whole pipeline on one page, ad to landing page to list to offer, with the four boxes waiting for your names in them. Don't spend a dollar until all four are filled.</p><a class="btn dark" href="/resources/starter-map">Get the Starter Map <span class="a">→</span></a></div>
    <p class="reveal">See how it fits together? The ad fills the list; the systems in Chapter Three run it. Ten dollars a day is not a shortcut. It is a metronome: enough spend to produce real data and real subscribers, small enough that no single bad day matters. The full paid-traffic build is The Ads Edit™, which sits inside Membership Premium alongside the automation module.</p>
    <div class="box mistake reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✕</span> Stated plainly</span><p>This assumes an offer that already sells. Ads amplify what is true, including what is broken. Send paid traffic at an offer nobody has bought and you will pay to learn what a free audience would have told you.</p></div>

    <div class="chapter reveal"><span class="bignum">07</span><div class="num">Chapter Seven</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="numbers">The four numbers beginners actually need</h2></div>
    <p class="answer reveal">Beginners need four ad numbers: click-through rate, cost per thousand impressions, cost per click, and return on ad spend.</p>
    <p class="reveal">Your ads dashboard will show you forty. Thirty-six of them exist to make you feel you need a course to understand the other four.</p>
    <p class="reveal"><strong>CTR, click-through rate.</strong> Of the people who saw the ad, how many clicked? This grades the <em>ad</em>. Below roughly 1% on cold traffic, the hook, image or promise isn't earning the click.</p>
    <p class="reveal"><strong>CPM, cost per thousand impressions.</strong> What you pay for attention. This grades the <em>audience</em>. A high CPM means you are bidding into an expensive room, and the fix is usually who you are targeting, not the creative.</p>
    <p class="reveal"><strong>CPC, cost per click.</strong> CTR and CPM, multiplied into a bill.</p>
    <p class="reveal"><strong>ROAS, return on ad spend.</strong> Revenue back for each dollar in. The only number that decides whether the machine keeps running.</p>
    <div class="box check reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✓</span> The diagnosis, in order</span><ul>
      <li>Impressions but no clicks → the ad is broken, not the page</li>
      <li>Clicks but no signups → the landing page is broken, not the ad</li>
      <li>Signups but no sales → the ad did its job; the sequence or the offer is the leak</li>
    </ul></div>
    <div class="box tip reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✦</span> Two rules that protect you from yourself</span><p><strong>The 72-hour rule:</strong> touch nothing for three days. The platform is calibrating, the early numbers are noise, and every nervous edit restarts the clock. <strong>The 10–20% rule:</strong> when something works, raise the budget 10–20% at a time. Double it overnight and you reset the calibration that made it work.</p></div>

    <div class="chapter left reveal"><span class="bignum">08</span><div class="num">Chapter Eight</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="loop">When automations become a system</h2></div>
    <p class="answer reveal">Automations become a system when they feed each other, and when you can see all of them on one page.</p>
    <p class="reveal">One automation is a convenience. Five are a collection. Automations that feed each other are something else: the ad that fills the list, the sequence that warms it, the morning task that gives every new member something to open, the sweep that makes sure nobody's first question goes unanswered, the weekly numbers that tell you which part is leaking. That is a business that runs on decisions instead of effort.</p>
    <p class="reveal">The difference has a name: the difference between a creator and a business owner. <strong>A creator owns tasks. A business owner owns systems.</strong> Automation is the tactic. Systems are the architecture.</p>

    <div class="notebook reveal">
      <div class="h">Field Notes — the loop</div>
      <p>The ad fills the list →</p>
      <p>the sequence warms the list →</p>
      <p>the morning task gives her a reason to open →</p>
      <p>the sweep answers her first question →</p>
      <p>and the weekly numbers say where it leaks.</p>
      <span class="pin-note">build it once ♡</span>
    </div>

    <p class="reveal">One last thing, because it is the part nobody warns you about. Once the machine runs, your job changes. You stop doing the tasks and start reading the reports. That feels like less work, and it is, but it is not no work. A system you never check is a system that has been failing politely for a month.</p>
    <p class="deeper reveal"><span>Go deeper —</span> <a href="/learn/business-that-runs-without-you">How to Build a Business That Runs Without You →</a></p>

    <div class="box action reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">→</span> Action Step</span><h3>Find the sixth time</h3><p>List every task you did more than five times in the last two weeks. Score each one on frequency, sameness and stakes. The top scorer is your first automation, and it is probably your welcome sequence. Then write its instruction the way Chapter Four describes: sources, what done looks like, the check, and what to do when it can't. Predictable is the point.</p><a class="btn dark" href="/membership">See the Full Curriculum <span class="a">→</span></a></div>

